% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 3 "The Fox and the Crow", section 3.1
%         ("The fox's goal and beliefs").
% Last verification: no open issues flagged (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The fox's beliefs are plain Head-if-Body conditionals. The general ones range
% over any animal; the one about where the fox stands names the fox and the
% crow, as the book's "I" and "the crow" do. The book writes the crow's initial possession and the
% fox's achieved possession with the same word "has"; to keep the rules free of
% recursion we use "holds" for the primitive (initial) possession that the crow
% starts with, and "has" for the possession the fox reasons towards.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*an animal* holds *an object*.
*an animal* has *an object*.
*an animal* is near *an object*.
*an animal* picks up *an object*.
*an animal* sings.
*an animal* praises *a second animal*.

the knowledge base fox and crow includes:

% An animal that already holds an object has it.
an animal has an object
    if the animal holds the object.

% "An animal has an object if it is near the object and picks it up."
an animal has an object
    if the animal is near the object
    and the animal picks up the object.

% "I am near the cheese if the crow has the cheese and the crow sings." This is
% the fox's belief about itself: it holds because the fox stands under the
% crow's branch, so whatever the singing crow drops lands by the fox. It does
% not generalize to any animal (e.g. to whoever praises the crow), so "I" and
% "the crow" stay the fox and the crow.
fox is near an object
    if crow holds the object
    and crow sings.

% "the crow sings if I praise the crow."
an animal sings
    if a second animal praises the animal.

scenario story is:
    crow holds cheese.
    fox praises crow.
    fox picks up cheese.
    possession expects answers ["crow has cheese", "fox has cheese"].

% Praising the crow does not bring a bystander near the cheese: the crow sings
% and drops it by the fox, not by the wolf.
scenario bystander is:
    crow holds cheese.
    wolf praises crow.
    wolf picks up cheese.
    possession expects answers ["crow has cheese"].

query possession is:
    which animal has which object.
